{"data": {"event": {"id": "c84ce7b8-396a-4550-a70a-3c8abad28060", "picture": "https://services.medicalcongress.online/congress/medias/2026/JFR2026/1974/video/thumbs/poster.jpg", "sessionType": "S\u00e9ance p\u00e9dagogique", "sessionTypeColor": "#44bdfa", "voting": null, "title": "Abdominal emergencies: A focus on the imaging findings that alter patient management", "start": "2026-10-08T14:00+02:00", "end": "2026-10-08T15:30+02:00", "objectives": ["<div>At the conclusion of this didactic session, participants will be able to:<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> </span></div>", "<div>1. Analyze the imaging spectrum of mesenteric ischemia, distinguishing arterial, venous, and non-occlusive etiologies, and identify radiologic features predictive of transmural necrosis that necessitate urgent surgical or endovascular intervention.<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> </span></div>", "<div>2. Critically evaluate imaging findings in bowel obstruction, differentiating simple from strangulated obstruction, and recognize CT signs associated with ischemia, closed-loop obstruction, or impending perforation that mandate emergent operative management.<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> </span></div>", "<div>3. Interpret imaging in abdominal trauma according to current management paradigms, identifying imaging biomarkers that guide non-operative management, embolization, or immediate surgical exploration.<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> </span></div>", "<div>4. Assess imaging strategies for lower gastrointestinal bleeding, recognizing active contrast extravasation&nbsp; and integrating radiologic findings into multidisciplinary decision-making involving endoscopic, interventional radiologic, or surgical treatment.<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> </span></div>"], "onAir": null, "vod": {"checkPermissions": ["media:view"], "media": null}, "broadcasts": {"items": []}, "room": {"id": "25554d0b-a773-41ee-8a5a-92070d741b5d", "name": "243", "type": "ROOM"}, "schedule": {"items": [{"id": "0ed279ce-e9